WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.413
Work the build in order.

2
00:00:01.413 --> 00:00:04.085
Step zero isn't optional
and can't be moved:

3
00:00:04.085 --> 00:00:08.408
configuring approval before
emergency access accounts exist is

4
00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:12.338
the one sequence that can lock
an agency out of its own tenant.

5
00:00:12.338 --> 00:00:13.975
Microsoft documents it.

6
00:00:13.975 --> 00:00:18.742
Every Privileged Role and Global
Administrator assignment is eligible,

7
00:00:18.742 --> 00:00:22.258
approval is required, and no
approvers are configured,

8
00:00:22.258 --> 00:00:26.400
so the default approvers, the
active administrators, don't exist.

9
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.857
Avoid it with both halves of the fix:
emergency accounts with permanent active

10
00:00:30.857 --> 00:00:35.588
Global Administrator, and named approver
groups on every role that needs approval.

11
00:00:35.588 --> 00:00:39.025
Step one builds those approver
groups, one per tier.

12
00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:44.106
Step two builds authentication context
c1 and its Conditional Access policy:

13
00:00:44.106 --> 00:00:49.421
the separate-device strength and a compliant
device, sign-in frequency every time,

14
00:00:49.421 --> 00:00:54.188
excluding the emergency group, and
turned On, because Microsoft requires

15
00:00:54.188 --> 00:00:58.800
the policy to exist and be enabled
before the context is selected in PIM.

16
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.100
Three constraints.

17
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.693
Don't scope that policy
to a directory role,

18
00:01:02.693 --> 00:01:06.100
because at activation the user
doesn't hold the role yet.

19
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:11.507
The fallback to plain MFA happens only
when no policy targets the context;

20
00:01:11.507 --> 00:01:16.825
it doesn't trigger if the policy is off,
in report-only or excludes the user.

21
00:01:16.825 --> 00:01:22.303
And the context governs
activation, not use, so CA003,

22
00:01:22.303 --> 00:01:28.163
CA004 and CA025 still apply
to every privileged sign-in.

23
00:01:28.163 --> 00:01:30.866
Steps three and four apply role settings,

24
00:01:30.866 --> 00:01:34.762
in the portal for one role and
in Microsoft Graph for thirty.

25
00:01:34.762 --> 00:01:37.188
Each role's policy has seventeen rules.

26
00:01:37.188 --> 00:01:42.256
Step five converts standing assignments:
create the eligible assignment first,

27
00:01:42.256 --> 00:01:45.963
confirm the person can activate,
then remove the active one.

28
00:01:45.963 --> 00:01:48.638
Step six closes a gap PIM leaves.

29
00:01:48.638 --> 00:01:52.032
PIM protects becoming a
Conditional Access Administrator,

30
00:01:52.032 --> 00:01:55.425
but not editing a policy once
someone holds that permission.

31
00:01:55.425 --> 00:02:00.325
Protected actions attach a second
context, c2, to those permissions.

32
00:02:00.325 --> 00:02:03.961
Steps seven to nine are
notifications and alerts,

33
00:02:03.961 --> 00:02:06.940
reviews, and log export,
on the next slide.
